### What is the K-way merge pattern?

Given K sorted linked lists, merge them into one sorted list. The K-way merge pattern exploits the existing order: instead of concatenating all N nodes and re-sorting (O(N log N)), you only ever compare K current heads at a time using a min-heap.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TxnNode {
    public String branchId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public long timestamp;
    public TxnNode next;

    public TxnNode(String branchId, BigDecimal amount, long timestamp) {
        this.branchId = branchId;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.timestamp = timestamp;
    }
}
```

### How does the heap-based approach work?

Seed a `PriorityQueue` with each list's head. Loop: poll the smallest, attach to result, push its successor. The heap never exceeds K entries.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TxnNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KWayMerger {
    public static TxnNode mergeKLists(TxnNode[] lists) {
        PriorityQueue<TxnNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Long.compare(a.timestamp, b.timestamp)
        );

        for (TxnNode head : lists) {
            if (head != null) heap.offer(head);
        }

        TxnNode dummy = new TxnNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO, 0);
        TxnNode tail = dummy;

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            TxnNode smallest = heap.poll();
            tail.next = smallest;
            tail = tail.next;
            if (smallest.next != null) {
                heap.offer(smallest.next);
            }
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TxnNode l1 = new TxnNode("HDFC", new BigDecimal("1000"), 100);
        l1.next = new TxnNode("HDFC", new BigDecimal("3000"), 300);

        TxnNode l2 = new TxnNode("ICICI", new BigDecimal("2000"), 200);
        l2.next = new TxnNode("ICICI", new BigDecimal("4000"), 400);

        TxnNode l3 = new TxnNode("SBI", new BigDecimal("2500"), 250);

        TxnNode merged = mergeKLists(new TxnNode[]{l1, l2, l3});
        TxnNode cur = merged;
        while (cur != null) {
            System.out.println(cur.branchId + " t=" + cur.timestamp + " amt=" + cur.amount);
            cur = cur.next;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
HDFC t=100 amt=1000
ICICI t=200 amt=2000
SBI t=250 amt=2500
HDFC t=300 amt=3000
ICICI t=400 amt=4000
```

### Why is the heap bounded at K?

The heap holds at most one node per active list. When you poll a node, you push at most one successor from the same list. So the size never exceeds K, keeping each operation at O(log K).

```text
Heap state (K=3):
  Start:       [t=100, t=200, t=250]    size=3
  Poll t=100:  [t=200, t=250, t=300]    size=3 (pushed t=300)
  Poll t=200:  [t=250, t=300, t=400]    size=3 (pushed t=400)
  Poll t=250:  [t=300, t=400]           size=2 (SBI exhausted)
```

### How does the divide-and-conquer alternative work?

Pair adjacent lists and merge them in log K rounds. This achieves O(N log K) time with O(1) extra space because it only relinks existing nodes.

```java
public static TxnNode mergeKDivideConquer(TxnNode[] lists) {
    if (lists == null || lists.length == 0) return null;
    int interval = 1;
    while (interval < lists.length) {
        for (int i = 0; i + interval < lists.length; i += interval * 2) {
            lists[i] = mergeTwoLists(lists[i], lists[i + interval]);
        }
        interval *= 2;
    }
    return lists[0];
}
```

### What is the complexity comparison?

| Approach | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Min-heap K-way merge | O(N log K) | O(K) |
| Divide-and-conquer pairwise | O(N log K) | O(1) |
| Sequential pairwise | O(N * K) | O(1) |
| Concatenate and sort | O(N log N) | O(N) |

### What are the edge cases?

Empty array, array of all nulls, single list, and lists of vastly different lengths.

```java
System.out.println(KWayMerger.mergeKLists(new TxnNode[]{}));       // null
System.out.println(KWayMerger.mergeKLists(new TxnNode[]{null}));   // null
```

### Why use the dummy head pattern?

The dummy head eliminates special-casing the first node. Every attachment uses the same `tail.next = node` pattern, and the result starts at `dummy.next`.

```java
TxnNode dummy = new TxnNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO, 0);
TxnNode tail = dummy;
// ... loop appends to tail.next uniformly ...
return dummy.next; // skip the dummy
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The K-way merge pattern appears in external sorting (merging K sorted files from disk), database query execution (merging sorted index scans), and stream processing. The heap approach streams naturally, which fits feed-based banking systems.

In production at National Bank of India, the central ledger merges K branch transaction feeds, each pre-sorted by timestamp, into one globally ordered stream.

```java
// Production streaming merge
while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    TxnNode next = heap.poll();
    ledgerWriter.write(next);
    if (next.next != null) heap.offer(next.next);
}
```
